  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nAs of 2 PM Monday afternoon, midlevel ridging extends from the \nsouthern Plains through the upper MS valley. A strong midlevel \ntrough was coming ashore across southern CA with Hurricane Polo \nnearing landfall in the central Baja of California. At the surface, \na trough axis continues to deepen across the high Plains with the \napproaching western trough. Transitioning into tonight, a 30-40 kt \nLLJ will begin to transport a tropical airmass, due in part to \nHurricane Polo, across the entire forecast area as PW near 1.75\" by \nTuesday morning. PW values will only increase further (up to 2.1\") \nby Tuesday afternoon. Increasing diffluence aloft along with the \npassage of a speed max across northeast CO/NE will produce \nwidespread shower and thunderstorm activity by late afternoon with \nthe greatest chances across central and south-central KS. Forecast \nsounding depict MLCAPE up to 1000-1500 J/kg with modestly veered \nhodographs yielding around 30 kt of effective shear. As a result, a \nfew strong or severe thunderstorms cannot be entirely ruled out. The \nstrongest storms may produce hail up to quarter size and heavy \nrainfall. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms will continue into the nighttime hours \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning as strong low/midlevel WAA \noverlap with increasing midlevel height falls. The abnormally moist \nairmass will continue to support heavy rainfall, especially across \ncentral KS. The main midlevel low will eject into the central Plains \nWednesday afternoon with continued rounds of heavy rainfall as\nchances spread eastwards into southeast KS. In fact, the \npotential for a few severe storms will increase Wednesday \nafternoon across central, south-central, and possibly southeast \nKS. A Pacific front/dryline will progress across the area during\nthe afternoon hours. The prefrontal environment will remain very\nmoist with near moist adiabatic lapse rates throughout the\ncolumn resulting in low instability values (a few hundred J/kg \nat most). Shear, on the other hand, will be quite impressive \nwith long, veering hodographs throughout the profile (~40 kt of\neffective shear). Trends will need to be monitored as a low \nCAPE/high shear setup may emerge with the potential for low- \ntopped supercells.\n\nAs the midlevel low/trough slides through the area late Thursday \ninto Thursday night, deformation precipitation will progress through \nthe entire area. The greatest rain totals are expected to remain \nacross portions of central and southeast KS. Opted to hoist a Flood \nWatch for Lincoln and Russell counties from 7 PM Tuesday through 7 \nPM Thursday. This particular area was included in the Flood\nWatch due to recent rainfall (1-2\" over the past week)\noverlapping with the greatest potential for additional rainfall\nup to 3+\". Considered a Flood Watch for southeast KS but a lack\nof recent rainfall (generally less than 1\" over the last 7 \ndays) decreased confidence in flooding concerns. That being \nsaid, an expansion of the watch is certainly possible with later\nforecast issuances. \n\nFor Friday and beyond, midlevel heights will increase as western \nCONUS ridge axis builds into the Plains. This should yield drier \nconditions but seasonable temperatures in the 70s.",
